{"qid":"Q160440","names":["Crimean Khanate"],"title":"Crimean Khanate","endonym":{"native":"Qırım Hanlığı","lang":"crh"},"capsule":"The Crimean Khanate was a Crimean Tatar state founded c. 1441 by Hacı I Giray, who broke from the fragmenting Golden Horde and established a dynasty that would rule the peninsula and the northern Black Sea steppe for over three centuries. After 1475 the khanate became an Ottoman protectorate, with the Giray dynasty providing cavalry armies crucial to Ottoman campaigns as far as Astrakhan and Poland-Lithuania; the khans also conducted regular slave raids into Ukraine and Russia that brought hundreds of thousands of captives to Ottoman markets. The khanate's independence eroded as Russia expanded southward; Catherine II formally annexed Crimea in 1783 following the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca (1774), ending the last major successor state of the Golden Horde.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The khanate's core Crimean peninsula is precisely defined; the steppe territory north of the peninsula over which the khans exercised nomadic dominion fluctuated seasonally and is represented only approximately.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Alan Fisher, 'The Crimean Tatars' (Hoover Institution Press, 1978)","url":"https://archive.org/details/crimeantatar00fish"},{"label":"Brian Boeck, 'Imperial Boundaries: Cossack Communities and Empire-Building in the Age of Peter the Great' (Cambridge UP, 2009)","url":"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/imperial-boundaries/4E7E7D7D1E0C9A16A1B1A5E7C1A5A5E7"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Crimean Khanate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimean_Khanate"}